---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import SectionNav from "../components/SectionNav.astro";
import LevelSection from "../components/LevelSection.astro";
import { levels, type NodeType } from "../data/levels";

// Each device type is introduced once, above the first level that uses it —
// which is what the pop-up hints used to do, except now it stays on the page.
const seen = new Set<NodeType>();
const sections = levels.map((level, index) => {
  const newDevices = [...new Set(level.nodes.map((node) => node.type))].filter(
    (type) => !seen.has(type),
  );
  for (const type of newDevices) {
    seen.add(type);
  }
  const nextLevel = levels[index + 1];
  return {
    level,
    newDevices,
    next: nextLevel ? { id: nextLevel.id, title: nextLevel.title } : null,
  };
});
---

<Layout title="Can You Break the Internet?">
  <Header />
  <SectionNav />

  <main>
    <section id="intro" class="intro">
      <p class="intro-lead">
        The internet doesn't survive because nothing breaks. It survives because things are allowed
        to break — a link fails, and traffic takes another way round.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every level below is a network you can attack. <strong
          >Click any link to cut it</strong
        > — the diagram reroutes live and the status line tells you what's still standing. Keyboard:
        focus a diagram, use the arrow keys to move between devices and links, and Enter or Space to
        cut. On a touchscreen you can pinch to zoom and drag with a thumb to move around. A level is
        complete once every route to every server is gone; levels get harder as the networks get more
        like the real thing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Watch closely and you'll see the dark dot — your request — travel out, and only once it
        lands does the hollow dot set off back: the answer, worked out as its own independent trip
        rather than the outbound path just played backwards (though in these diagrams the cheapest
        way back does happen to be the cheapest way out, reversed — real IP traffic isn't guaranteed
        that). The ms next to each link is a simulated one-way delay, not a measurement of any real
        connection, and a real page is rarely just one such round trip — a browser fires off many
        requests in parallel, some answered straight from a cache. These are simplified teaching
        models, not a literal map of how the internet works: real networks carry far more redundancy
        and nuance than five diagrams can show, and even a well-built one isn't immune to outages —
        redundancy lowers the odds of a single point of failure, it doesn't guarantee uptime.
      </p>
    </section>

    {sections.map((props) => <LevelSection {...props} />)}
  </main>

  <script src="../scripts/main.ts"></script>
</Layout>
